**Table of Contents**

**1. Overview & Design Goals**

> **Release Target:**
>
> Cross-tenant collaboration is a v1.1 feature. The database schema and
> API foundations are laid in v1.0, but the feature is not
> user-accessible until v1.1. This document specifies the complete
> design so v1.0 development does not create structural barriers to v1.1
> delivery.

Cross-tenant collaboration is the platform\'s defining differentiator.
No major competitor offers true multi-tenant collaboration where two
independent companies --- a General Contractor and a Subcontractor ---
each operate their own full platform environment and selectively share
project data across a governed bridge.

This document specifies the complete design: use cases, data flow,
permission model, UI/UX workflows, API contracts, security model, and
edge cases.

**1.1 Design Goals**

-   Each tenant remains completely independent --- no data merges, no
    shared tables, no loss of data sovereignty

-   The collaboration bridge is opt-in --- both parties must consent
    before any data flows

-   The data that crosses the bridge is explicitly governed --- neither
    party sees more than what was agreed

-   The GC sees their project as a unified whole even though sub data
    crosses from another tenant

-   The Sub sees the GC project as a job in their own dashboard
    alongside their other independent work

-   The connection can be dissolved cleanly --- both parties retain
    their own records after disconnect

-   The architecture is extensible --- designed for 2-party connections
    but capable of N-party in future

**1.2 The Problem Being Solved**

Today, a GC manages a project with 8 subcontractors using a mix of
email, spreadsheets, text messages, and a single-tenant PM tool. The
problems:

-   Subs don\'t have visibility into the GC\'s schedule --- they show up
    when told, often too early or too late

-   GC has no real-time visibility into sub progress --- they must make
    phone calls for status updates

-   Sub invoices are submitted via email, approved via email, tracked in
    a spreadsheet

-   Lien waivers are paper documents --- collected inconsistently, often
    missing at closeout

-   RFIs bounce back and forth in email --- no central log, frequent
    duplication

-   When a sub\'s work fails inspection, the GC learns about it via
    phone call, hours or days later

Cross-tenant collaboration solves all of these --- in real time, with
both parties in their own native environment, without either party
having to adopt a foreign tool or share sensitive internal data.

**2. Use Cases**

**2.1 Primary Use Case: GC Managing Trade Subs**

ABC General Contractors is building a 12-unit apartment complex. They
have awarded scopes to:

-   Smith Electrical Co. --- Electrical (tenant on platform)

-   Martinez Plumbing LLC --- Plumbing (tenant on platform)

-   Northeast Framing Inc. --- Framing (not on platform --- free portal)

Smith Electrical and Martinez Plumbing are full platform tenants. Each
has their own company environment, their own projects, their own
financials, and their own team. When ABC GC connects with them on the
apartment project:

-   The apartment job appears in Smith Electrical\'s dashboard as one of
    their active jobs

-   Smith\'s electricians clock in and out against the GC\'s project ---
    those hours flow into Smith\'s own payroll and job costing

-   When Smith completes a rough-in phase, ABC GC sees it update on
    their project dashboard in real time

-   When the rough electrical fails inspection, both ABC GC\'s PM and
    Smith Electric\'s PM are notified simultaneously

-   Smith submits their draw invoice through the platform --- ABC GC
    reviews and approves without leaving their dashboard

-   Northeast Framing (not a tenant) accesses a free limited portal ---
    submits daily reports and invoices, but has no full platform
    environment

**2.2 Secondary Use Case: Trade Prime with Specialty Sub**

Smith Electrical (a tenant) wins a prime contract on a commercial
building. They sub out the fire alarm work to a specialty contractor.
Smith acts as the prime --- they initiate the connection and the
specialty sub receives a portal invite or platform connection. Same
architecture, same data governance --- the \'GC\' role is held by Smith
in this context.

**2.3 Scope Boundary: What Is NOT Cross-Tenant Collaboration**

-   A GC adding a vendor (sub) to their vendor database --- this is
    single-tenant, no data bridge

-   A GC creating a \'subcontractor\' record and tracking their scope
    internally --- still single-tenant

-   A client accessing their project portal --- this is a portal user
    within the GC\'s tenant, not a separate tenant

-   A GC inviting a sub who is not a platform tenant to a free portal
    --- this is the Sub Portal feature, not full cross-tenant
    collaboration

**3. Connection Model**

**3.1 Connection States**

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **State**          **Description**        **Who Can          **Data Access**
                                            Transition**       
  ------------------ ---------------------- ------------------ ---------------------
  **Pending**        GC has sent connection Sub accepts or     No data flows yet ---
                     request --- Sub has    declines; GC can   invitation metadata
                     not yet responded      cancel             only

  **Active**         Both parties have      Either party can   Governed data flows
                     accepted --- shared    close/disconnect   in real time per
                     project layer is live                     permissions matrix

  **Completed**      Project closeout ---   GC PM initiates,   Shared data archived
                     connection formally    Sub confirms       for both parties ---
                     completed by GC PM                        read-only bridge

  **Disconnected**   Either party ended the Either party can   Bridge closed ---
                     connection             disconnect at any  each party retains
                                            time               their own archived
                                                               copy of shared events

  **Rejected**       Sub declined the       Sub only           No data ever flowed
                     connection request                        --- request archived
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**3.2 Connection Initiation Flow**

1.  GC PM opens a project and navigates to the Subcontractors section

2.  GC PM selects a vendor from their vendor database or enters a new
    company name + email

3.  Platform checks: does an active tenant exist with this email domain
    or company name match?

    -   If YES → displays \'This company appears to be on \[PLATFORM
        NAME\]. Send a platform connection request?\' with brief
        explanation

    -   If NO → defaults to portal invite (free limited portal flow)

4.  GC PM defines the connection scope: which project, brief scope
    description, and contract value (optional)

5.  GC PM selects data sharing permissions --- pre-populated with
    sensible defaults (see Section 5)

6.  Platform generates a connection request --- creates a
    tenant_connections record with status=\'pending\'

7.  Sub tenant\'s Owner and any PMs receive an in-app notification and
    email: \'\[GC Company\] has invited \[Sub Company\] to collaborate
    on \[Project Name\] via \[PLATFORM NAME\]\'

8.  Sub reviews: project name, scope description, GC company profile,
    and proposed data sharing permissions

9.  Sub accepts or declines:

    -   Accept → status=\'active\', shared project layer instantiated,
        job appears in Sub\'s dashboard

    -   Decline → status=\'rejected\', GC notified, GC defaults to
        portal invite flow if desired

**3.3 Connection Discovery --- Matching Logic**

The platform uses fuzzy matching to identify potential tenant
connections when a GC enters a subcontractor:

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Signal**             **Confidence**   **Action**
  ---------------------- ---------------- ------------------------------------
  Email domain exact     High             Prompt for platform connection with
  match (e.g.                             high confidence message
  smith-electric.com)                     

  Company name exact or  Medium           Prompt with lower confidence: \'Did
  very close match                        you mean Smith Electrical Co. on
  (Levenshtein distance                   \[PLATFORM NAME\]?\'
  \< 2)                                   

  Email address direct   Very High        \'John Smith at Smith Electrical is
  match to existing user                  already on \[PLATFORM NAME\].
                                          Connect directly?\'

  No match found         ---              Default to portal invite --- no
                                          platform connection prompt
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

> **Privacy Note:**
>
> The matching process only reveals that a tenant with a matching
> name/domain exists on the platform --- it does not expose any details
> about that tenant\'s business, projects, or users. The GC sees only:
> company name, trade type, and a \'verified platform tenant\' badge.

**4. Data Governance Model**

This is the most architecturally critical section of the spec. The data
governance model defines exactly what crosses the tenant boundary, how
it flows, and what data sovereignty guarantees each party has.

**4.1 The Golden Rule**

> **Golden Rule:**
>
> Sub\'s internal business data NEVER lives in the GC\'s tenant tables.
> GC\'s internal business data NEVER lives in the Sub\'s tenant tables.
> All shared data lives in a neutral shared_project_events bridge table
> that neither tenant \'owns\'. Both tenants read from this bridge.
> Neither tenant can modify the other\'s native records.

**4.2 Data Flow Architecture**

> ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
>
> │ GC TENANT (t_001) │ SUB TENANT (t_002) │
>
> │ │ │
>
> │ project.id = p_100 │ project.id = p_200 │
>
> │ (GC\'s internal project) │ (Sub\'s internal project record │
>
> │ │ created when connection │
>
> │ │ accepted) │
>
> │ │ │ │ │
>
> │ │ WRITES │ WRITES │ │
>
> │ ▼ │ ▼ │
>
> │ ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
>
> │ │ SHARED PROJECT EVENTS BRIDGE │ │
>
> │ │ connection_id = c_500 │ │
>
> │ │ (neutral --- belongs to neither tenant) │ │
>
> │ │ │ │
>
> │ │ event: task_completed (source: t_002) │ │
>
> │ │ event: inspection_pass (source: t_002) │ │
>
> │ │ event: invoice_submit (source: t_002) │ │
>
> │ │ event: daily_report (source: t_002) │ │
>
> │ │ event: rfi_response (source: t_002) │ │
