**6. Sub\'s Dashboard Experience**

When a Sub accepts a connection, the GC project appears in the Sub\'s
own dashboard as a job --- indistinguishable in structure from any
project the Sub created themselves. This is a critical UX principle: the
Sub uses the platform exactly the same way for GC-connected jobs as for
their own direct jobs.

**6.1 Sub\'s Project Record**

When a connection is accepted, a project record is created in the Sub\'s
tenant:

> // Created in Sub\'s tenant (tenant_id = t_002)
>
> projects table:
>
> id = p_200 (Sub\'s own project UUID)
>
> tenant_id = t_002
>
> name = \'Main St Office --- Electrical (ABC General Contractors)\'
>
> job_type = \'subcontract_work\'
>
> status = \'active\'
>
> source = \'cross_tenant_connection\'
>
> connection_id = c_500 // Link to tenant_connections record

This is a real project in the Sub\'s tenant --- not a read-only view
into the GC\'s tenant. The Sub\'s project is their own. They manage
their scope, their crew, their costs, and their invoices from this
project record.

**6.2 What the Sub Sees in Their Dashboard**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Dashboard      **What Sub Sees**         **Source**
  Section**                                  
  ---------------- ------------------------- ----------------------------
  Project overview Project name, GC company, From connection record +
                   their scope, contract     Sub\'s own project
                   value, status             

  Schedule         Their phases and tasks    Shared bridge + Sub\'s own
                   --- pulled from shared    task tracking
                   task assignments          

  My scope         Their assigned work       Shared from GC via bridge on
                   breakdown from the GC\'s  connection init
                   project                   

  Daily reports    Their own daily reports   Sub\'s own tenant records
                   for this job              

  Time & labor     Their crew clock-ins      Sub\'s own tenant records
                   against this job, total   
                   hours                     

  Inspections      Inspections for their     Shared bridge events
                   scope --- pass/fail       
                   history                   

  Invoices         Their invoices to GC ---  Sub\'s own records + GC
                   status, approval, payment approval events from bridge

  Change orders    COs issued to them by GC  Shared bridge events
                   --- their responses       

  Documents /      Plans and documents       Read access to GC-shared
  plans            shared by GC for their    documents via bridge
                   scope                     

  RFIs             RFIs relevant to their    Sub\'s own + bridge events
                   scope                     

  Submittals       Their submittals for      Sub\'s own records + GC
                   their scope               review events from bridge

  Messages         Project thread with GC    Bridge messages --- filtered
                   team                      to this connection

  Lien waivers     Waiver requests from GC + Bridge events + Sub\'s own
                   their signed copies       signed documents
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**6.3 Sub\'s Job Costing for GC Work**

When a Sub is working on a GC-connected project, their internal job
costing runs normally against their own tenant:

-   Time entries clock in against Sub\'s project p_200 --- captured in
    Sub\'s time_entries table

-   Material costs entered by Sub\'s office --- captured in Sub\'s
    budget_line_items

-   Sub runs their own job costing reports: labor hours × burdened rate
    vs. their contract value with GC

-   The GC sees NONE of this --- GC only sees total labor hours if the
    Sub has enabled that permission

-   Sub\'s profit margin on the GC job is completely invisible to the GC

**7. GC\'s Dashboard Experience**

The GC\'s project dashboard presents a unified view of the project even
though some data originates from Sub tenants. The GC experiences this as
a single coherent project --- not as a patchwork of connections.

**7.1 Unified Project View**

On the GC\'s project dashboard, connected sub scopes appear as part of
the project naturally:

-   Schedule: Sub phase completions appear as task completions on the
    GC\'s Gantt --- same visual treatment as self-performed work

-   Budget: Sub contract values appear as committed costs in the GC\'s
    budget --- invoice approvals update actual costs

-   Subcontractors panel: Each connected sub shows real-time status ---
    current phase, last activity, open items count

-   Inspections: All inspections across all scopes (self-performed and
    sub) on one inspection log

-   Daily reports: GC\'s own reports plus sub reports aggregated into a
    single project daily log

-   Documents: Sub-uploaded submittals and RFIs appear in the project
    document center

**7.2 What the GC Does NOT See**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Hidden Data**        **Reason**
  ---------------------- ------------------------------------------------
  Sub\'s labor costs and Sub\'s internal financials are private --- GC
  pay rates              sees only contract value and invoice amounts

  Sub\'s profit margin   Commercially sensitive --- never shared
  on the job             

  Sub\'s other active    Completely separate tenants --- no
  projects               cross-visibility

  Sub\'s internal team   Private company data --- GC only sees who showed
  or employee list       up if daily_report hours sharing is enabled

  Sub\'s client          Private to Sub tenant
  relationships          

  Sub\'s QuickBooks or   Private --- integrations are per-tenant
  other integrations     

  Sub\'s overhead or     Private financial data --- always hidden
  markup                 
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**8. Connection Lifecycle & Closeout**

**8.1 Normal Closeout Flow**

10. Project reaches substantial completion --- GC PM initiates closeout

11. Platform generates a closeout checklist for the Sub\'s scope:

    -   All lien waivers signed and received

    -   Final invoice submitted and approved

    -   All punch list items verified

    -   All submittals approved

    -   All open RFIs closed

12. GC PM marks the connection as \'completed\'

13. Sub receives notification --- confirms closeout

14. Connection status moves to \'completed\'

15. Shared project events bridge archived --- both tenants retain a
    read-only copy

16. Sub\'s project p_200 moves to \'completed\' status in their
    dashboard

17. GC\'s project retains all shared history for closeout record-keeping

**8.2 Early Disconnection (Scope Termination)**

Either party can disconnect at any time --- for scope termination,
dispute, or other reasons:

-   Disconnecting party selects \'Disconnect\' with a reason code:
    completed \| terminated_for_cause \| mutual_agreement \| other

-   Both parties receive immediate notification of the disconnection

-   Shared bridge is archived --- no new events can be written to it

-   Both parties retain their own copy of the bridge event history

-   Sub\'s project p_200 moves to status=\'cancelled\' or \'completed\'
    depending on reason code

-   GC\'s project scope shows the sub as disconnected --- internal cost
    records preserved

**8.3 What Happens to Data After Disconnection**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Data Type**      **GC Retains**     **Sub Retains**
  ------------------ ------------------ ---------------------------------
  Shared project     Yes --- read-only  Yes --- read-only archive in
  event history      archive            their project record

  Invoices submitted Yes --- in their   Yes --- in their own invoice
  and paid           financial records  records

  Signed lien        Yes --- stored in  Yes --- copy in Sub\'s document
  waivers            GC\'s document     storage
                     storage            

  Approved change    Yes --- in GC\'s   Yes --- in Sub\'s CO record
  orders             CO record          

  Inspection results Yes --- in GC\'s   Yes --- in Sub\'s inspection log
                     inspection log     

  Sub\'s internal    No --- never had   Yes --- always in Sub\'s own
  labor data         access             tenant

  Sub\'s profit      No --- never had   Yes --- always in Sub\'s own
  margin / costs     access             tenant

  Sub\'s other       No --- never had   Yes --- fully private to Sub
  projects           access             
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**9. Security & Privacy Model**

**9.1 Cross-Tenant Data Access Security**

Cross-tenant collaboration introduces a new challenge to the otherwise
simple RLS model: some data legitimately needs to cross tenant
boundaries. The security design:

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Security       **How Cross-Tenant Is Handled**
  Layer**          
  ---------------- ------------------------------------------------------
  Database RLS     The shared_project_events table uses
                   connection_id-based RLS --- only tenants that are
                   parties to a connection can read its events. No
                   tenant_id-based RLS (since events belong to a
                   connection, not a single tenant). Policy: WHERE
                   connection_id IN (SELECT id FROM tenant_connections
                   WHERE initiating_tenant_id = current_tenant OR
                   receiving_tenant_id = current_tenant)

  API Layer        A dedicated CrossTenantService handles all bridge
                   reads and writes. It validates the requesting tenant
                   is a party to the connection before any data access.
                   Standard tenant_id injection is bypassed for bridge
                   operations --- replaced by connection_id validation.

  Write Validation When a Sub writes an event (e.g. task_completed), the
                   service validates: (1) the connection is active, (2)
                   the Sub is the receiving_tenant_id on this connection,
                   (3) the task being updated is within the Sub\'s
                   assigned scope, (4) the event_type is permitted by the
                   connection\'s permissions JSONB.

  Read Validation  When the GC reads bridge events, the service
                   validates: (1) the GC is the initiating_tenant_id on
                   this connection, (2) the event_type is in the
                   permitted list per the permissions JSONB, (3) the
                   connection is active or archived (not rejected or
                   pending).
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**9.2 Data Minimization Principle**

Events written to the bridge carry the minimum data necessary. Examples:

-   A task_completed event carries: task reference key, completion
    timestamp, optional notes, optional photo URLs --- NOT the Sub\'s
    internal task ID (which would reveal internal structure), NOT the
    Sub\'s employee name who completed it, NOT the Sub\'s cost codes

-   A daily_report_submitted event carries: date, total manpower count,
    total hours, work performed summary --- NOT employee names, NOT
    individual hours by employee, NOT any labor cost data

-   An invoice_submitted event carries: invoice number, period, total
    amount --- NOT the Sub\'s cost breakdown, NOT their markup, NOT
    their labor rates

**9.3 Connection Attempt Logging**

-   All connection requests --- sent and received --- are logged in the
    tenant audit trail for both parties

-   Rejected connections are logged --- the GC cannot spam connection
    requests to the same Sub

-   After 3 rejected requests to the same Sub, further requests require
    Owner-level authorization

-   All bridge events are immutable once written --- cannot be edited or
    deleted by either party

**10. API Specification**

**10.1 Connection Management Endpoints**

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Method**   **Endpoint**                   **Auth**   **Description**
  ------------ ------------------------------ ---------- ---------------------------------
  GET          /connections                   JWT        List all active and pending
                                                         connections for the current
                                                         tenant --- both as initiator and
                                                         receiver

  POST         /connections/initiate          JWT        Initiate a connection request ---
                                                         project_id, target company
                                                         email/name, scope description,
                                                         permissions, contract value

  POST         /connections/:id/accept        JWT        Sub accepts a connection request
                                                         --- activates the connection

  POST         /connections/:id/decline       JWT        Sub declines --- logs reason,
                                                         notifies GC

  POST         /connections/:id/complete      JWT        GC initiates closeout ---
                                                         triggers closeout checklist

  POST         /connections/:id/disconnect    JWT        Either party disconnects ---
                                                         reason_code required

  GET          /connections/:id/status        JWT        Full connection status --- shared
                                                         project events summary, open
                                                         items, closeout checklist
                                                         progress

  PUT          /connections/:id/permissions   JWT        Update permission settings ---
                                                         both parties must re-accept on
                                                         significant changes

  GET          /connections/discover          JWT        Check if a company name or email
                                                         matches an existing tenant ---
                                                         returns confidence score, no PII
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

**10.2 Shared Project Events Endpoints**

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Method**   **Endpoint**                        **Auth**   **Description**
  ------------ ----------------------------------- ---------- ---------------------------------
  GET          /connections/:id/events             JWT        List all bridge events for a
                                                              connection --- filterable by
                                                              event_type, date range,
                                                              source_tenant

  POST         /connections/:id/events             JWT        Write a new event to the bridge
                                                              --- validated against permissions
                                                              and scope

  GET          /connections/:id/events/:event_id   JWT        Get specific event detail

  GET          /connections/:id/summary            JWT        Cross-tenant project summary ---
                                                              current phase per scope, open
                                                              item counts, last activity

  GET          /connections/:id/invoices           JWT        Invoice workflow --- sub\'s
                                                              invoices to GC with approval
                                                              status

  GET          /connections/:id/lien-waivers       JWT        Lien waiver status for this
                                                              connection

  GET          /connections/:id/messages           JWT        Message thread for this
                                                              connection

  POST         /connections/:id/messages           JWT        Send a message on this connection
                                                              thread
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

